# estige exit node — Oracle Linux package env file.
#
# Read by systemd's EnvironmentFile= for estige.service; this is the service
# manager's env chain, and it holds no configuration of its own (d236). The
# binary reads exactly one place it is configured from: the store directory at
# $XDG_DATA_HOME/estige. The unit sets that to /var/lib/estige/.local/share and
# creates /var/lib/estige as the service's StateDirectory; provision it once, as
# the estige user:
#
#   estige keygen -principal exit-home       # this host's key + certificate
#   estige public -f /var/lib/estige/.local/share/estige/prv/exit-home.der
#   estige key check                         # validate before enabling
#
# exit.json carries the admitted relays and the egress policy. The default
# posture routes IP traffic: only space that can never answer a connection is
# refused (d242), so a range is refused when the operator RESERVES it:
#   {"relays":[{"name":"relay_bazzite","public":"<cert>"}],
#    "egress":{"denyCidrs":["169.254.169.254/32"],"ports":[443]}}
# "allowCidrs" carves a range back out of a reservation, and
# "refuseIPLiterals": true restores the old refusal of address targets.
#
# Bind (docs/d110): a bare port number, unset = 8080 on all interfaces.
# The packaged unit has no CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE; a port below 1024 needs an
# operator drop-in that adds it (README).
# PORT=8080
#
# A second, capped-edge listener (docs/d222), same shape as PORT.
# PORT_CLOUDFLARE=8081
